"channel_name": "LIVE", "id": "911297424825721"}]}, "change_log": "## 1.18.0 \u2014 Achievements, Battery and Store Release Rollup \u2014 2026-08-19\n\nThis entry rolls up all release work since 1.15. The Microsoft Store profile advanced through 1.16.0, while the native Meta Quest and Meta Quest PC profiles advanced to 1.18.0.\n\n### Added\n\n- Added 11 achievements covering first flight, airtime, score milestones, photo capture, battery challenges, and full completion. Native Meta Quest and Meta Quest PC submit through the Meta Platform SDK; Xbox support provides 1,000 total Gamerscore when its GDK integration define is enabled.\n- Added achieved and unachieved artwork for every Meta achievement, canonical cross-platform achievement metadata, persistent completion state, and a retry queue for temporary platform submission failures.\n- Added a ten-minute flight battery simulation. Centred-stick hover consumes one full charge in ten minutes, each stick adds its own linear activity load, and motor power stops when the battery is depleted.\n- Added a persistent battery option to the start menu and made battery simulation enabled by default for new or reset settings on every build profile.\n- Added a camera-stable gameplay HUD for score and battery state. Flat-screen builds use a safe-corner overlay; immersive builds use a fixed-size stereo world-space panel, with the native Quest layout positioned two HUD heights lower.\n- Added Xbox as an explicit platform route, together with a combined Steam/Xbox player manual and platform-specific achievement gating.\n- Added complete Steam store artwork and Meta achievement artwork to the release assets.\n- Added separate Microsoft Store commands for a local development player and a release upload package. The upload command now builds the validated IL2CPP player and creates the unsigned Partner Center MSIX in one workflow.\n\n### Changed\n\n- Flight rotation now applies the configured rate response directly to body-local angular velocity, eliminating inertia-driven alternating angular-rate corrections while preserving stick-only Mode 2 physics.\n- OpenXR runtime input bindings now address controller usages rather than device-specific control names, improving Meta Touch compatibility across Quest and Quest Link.\n- Quest Touch remains authoritative until an optional gamepad or FPV radio produces deliberate input, preventing the permanently connected zeroed Android gamepad from taking control.\n- Gameplay headset tracking now uses one guarded manual visual-pose path after reference capture. Headlook and FPV camera tilt remain isolated from aircraft input and physics.\n- Removed or disabled scene controller-action and locomotion objects that could compete with drone controls, while retaining the appropriate menu UI interaction and headset tracking for immersive builds.\n- Expanded flight and head-tracking diagnostics with Rigidbody interpolation, inertia, angular rate, render rate, physics rate, and tracked-device state.\n- Microsoft Store local and release outputs now use separate directories, and MSIX packaging requires a release marker proving that the source player came from the validated non-development Store profile.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- Fixed intermittent PC-VR control and head-tracking regressions introduced while integrating achievements and shared platform services.\n- Fixed light-drone pitch, roll, and yaw response oscillating between physics steps after the sticks returned to centre.\n- Fixed native Quest flight input being replaced by a connected but idle Android gamepad device.\n- Fixed the score/battery HUD scaling or filling the screen incorrectly across different flat-screen resolutions and XR camera hierarchies.\n- Fixed Microsoft Store packaging paths that could reuse stale or mixed local/release player files.\n- Fixed native Meta Quest Ctrl+B builds being able to inherit a renamed Android package. Quest builds now force and validate the published identifier `com.yourname.vectorskiesvr` before player generation.\n\n### Release Status\n\n- Meta Quest and Meta Quest PC Build Profiles are version 1.18.0; the Microsoft Store Build Profile is version 1.16.0 within this rollup.\n- Runtime and Editor C# projects compile with no errors after the release-preparation changes.\n- Achievement definitions, artwork, event routing, platform gating, and retry behaviour are present in source. Store-dashboard configuration and signed-in device testing remain external release checks.\n- Battery mode is on by default for new installs and after Reset to Defaults. An existing saved player preference is intentionally preserved.\n\n### Known Issues\n\n- Xbox achievement submission remains disabled until `VSVR_XBOX_ACHIEVEMENTS_SDK` is added to the Xbox Build Profile and an authenticated GDK social provider is supplied.\n- Steam, Microsoft Store PC, and unconfigured Windows builds intentionally do not initialize or submit achievements.\n- Meta and Xbox achievement identifiers, values, artwork, and localization must exactly match their respective developer dashboards before production submission.\n- Native Quest HUD placement and the updated manual head-tracking path still require final headset visual verification.", "created_date": 1787164424, "id": "27842880222034284", "version": "1.18.0", "version_code": 2000, "release_status": "RELEASED", "status": "APPROVED", "binary_test_results": {"nodes": []}, "obb_binary": null, "asset_files": {"count": 0}, "debug_symbols": {"count": 0}, "__isAppBinaryWithFileAsset": "AndroidBinary"}]}}, "viewer": {"user": null}}}